xmalloc and xrealloc return a pointer.  Without a declaration the caller
assumes int, which loses the top half of the value on LP64.  The
declarations were guarded for OSF1 alone; guard them for any LP64 machine,
which is what the original guard was really standing in for.

--- src/tparam.c~	1995-02-17 07:58:11.000000000 +0000
+++ src/tparam.c
@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@ xrealloc (ptr, size)
   return tem;
 }
 #else
-#ifdef OSF1
+#if defined(OSF1) || defined(_LP64)
 extern long *xmalloc();
 extern long *xrealloc();
 #endif
